    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Retirement",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7531\n\nSPONSOR: GALLIVAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to authorize Patrick Humiston to receive certain service credit\nunder section 384-d of the retirement and social security law\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis legislation will authorize Officer Patrick Humiston, of the Buffalo\nPolice Department (BPD), to transfer a defined period of service credit\nhe accrued while a member of the Erie County Sheriff's Office uniformed\npatrol to the pension plan of his existing employer.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 establishes the authority to transfer a specified period of\ntime related to service credit.\n\nSection 2 requires past service costs to be borne by the City of\nBuffalo.\n\nSection 3 sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPatrick Humiston, due to circumstances beyond his control was laid-off\nfrom his position as a sworn officer with the Buffalo Police Department\n(BPD) in 2002, thereby requiring him to seek alternative employment. He\nwas subsequently hired as an Erie County Sheriff's Deputy for a period\nof approximately three years before being re-hired by the BPD on August\n21, 2006.\n\nThis bill is necessary based on the fact that the City of Buffalo inad-\nvertently passed over Officer Humiston for reinstatement as a BPD offi-\ncer when the re-hiring of officers took place. Importantly, this bill\naddresses the concerns raised by the Executive in his veto message (see\nMemo. No. 236 of 2019) by specifically prescribing the timeframe associ-\nated with said transfer - May 26, 2004 to August 21, 2006 - the period\nwhen Officer Humiston should have been restored to duty with the BPD and\nwhen his re-hire was effectuated.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: S.4725/A.6998 passed Senate/passed Assembly\n2017-18: S.8051/A.10765 passed Senate/referred to governmental employees\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nSee fiscal note.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe bill would take effect immediately.\n",
